#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158141} :activity/id #uuid "b01ba0f8-312d-5711-866c-f427e34e9a49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016 :topic/id #uuid "7a5cb63d-dea0-503e-adc1-145b6400b020" :topic/math-academy-id 2835} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158141 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158486 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to compute the double integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is a washer enclosed between two circles centered at $(0,0)$ with radii $1$ and $2,$ respectively, as shown below.\n\n![](images/47/4752982aaf806b9e3b9c4d28d7a92bd01c3ff6a68afcca4235061a01b2fbc86f.png)\n\nThe easiest way to solve this problem is to convert it to polar coordinates. Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf T$ as\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThe preimage of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the rectangular region $\\Delta,$ given by \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its preimage $\\Delta$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0cc6c7f92704365c3ce15b91d85f703330b767a01f1f773b014cf61620563a3.png)\n\n             \n\nThe change of variables formula for polar coordinates states \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating this integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }d\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot [{2}^{3}-{1}^{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\pi \\cdot 7 \\\\  & =\\frac{14\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D}{{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\\,\\mathrm d A}} = \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "26614628-ce8b-5afe-ac90-b277c7c0f887" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4466 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0aaacbc4-1148-50d7-a5cf-50570ae0a925" :step/math-academy-id 27487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158144}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157102 :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433780}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "a6d6c9e7-5ab7-5550-b9f5-44b7f15426b5" :step/math-academy-id 27488 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158143}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158143 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157103 :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433781}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"} :step/id #uuid "659f011d-8169-5c22-b20a-0f19271882cd" :step/math-academy-id 27489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158144 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157300 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157104 :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424331}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "cd3a2583-f9bf-5dcc-9d80-5572b021e950" :step/math-academy-id 27493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158147}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158487 :tutorial/content "Let's get some more practice at expressing regions in polar coordinates. \n\nConsider the region enclosed by the curve $x^2+(y-2)^2=4$ and the line $y=2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/df/dffcd94cc96c33d173704a63cb03fb0f0098bc67d6c66a855c16f59694bde8e1.png)\n\nThis region represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,2).$\n\nFirst, we recall the usual transformations from polar to Cartesian coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now use the above formulas to write the functions that bound our region in polar coordinates:\n\n- For $x^2+(y-2)^2 = 4,$  we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ ({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = 4\\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =2 \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ \n\nFirst of all, note that if we draw an arrow through the region from the origin, it enters the region via the curve $r=2\\csc\\theta$ and exits via $r=4\\sin\\theta \\mathbin{:}$  \n\n![](images/b0/b09ab4e4e08dfb056964947dc866b965940b761d70c945faf001446c1df7869f.png)\n\nSecondly, our curves intersect at the points $(-2,2)$ and $(2,2).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/6a/6a132d5f8c58ce60c794595ee80c57c5b9b02e630004808cd53a7677efd022e7.png)\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 2\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq 4\\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\Big\\}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "870d0884-b353-5292-b2e5-593725024502" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4472 :tutorial/title "Writing More Complex Regions in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "098b5c2c-68e3-5938-bf5f-621a688c8ba7" :step/math-academy-id 27531 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158142}}] :activity/title "Double Integrals Between Polar Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157490}]}
